chunks.content:
A negative `z-index` is valid CSS, but it can put a clip behind an opaque
ancestor background when its parent does not create a stacking context. For
example, this backdrop can be covered by the root's background:

```css
#root {
  position: relative;
  background: #0a0a0a;
}
#backdrop {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  background: #f5e6c8;
  z-index: -1;
}
```

Add one property to keep the backdrop above its parent's background and below
foreground siblings:

```css
#root {
  isolation: isolate;
}
```

`isolation: isolate` creates a local stacking context. The backdrop becomes
visible in the browser, `snapshot`, and `render`; siblings at `z-index: 0` or
above can still cover it. You can also use an earlier background sibling and
raise foreground elements with non-negative `z-index` values.

`hyperframes lint` reports `negative_z_index` as a warning based on CSS text.
It can still warn when an ancestor already creates a stacking context, so
verify the visible result rather than treating every negative value as invalid.

